WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00.390 --> 00:03.490
Preparation for communication is absolutely critical,

2
00:03.970 --> 00:06.923
but it’s often the most neglected part of it.

3
00:06.923 --> 00:13.090
So what is EPIC? EPIC is a communication framework — four elements to it. It’s very intuitive,

4
00:13.480 --> 00:17.005
very easy to remember, but it packs a really mighty punch.

5
00:17.005 --> 00:19.010
The E in EPIC stands for Empathy.

6
00:20.170 --> 00:22.132
Before you can chart a course with somebody,

7
00:22.132 --> 00:26.590
before you can map out how you’re going to get from where they are now to where you need them to be,

8
00:27.080 --> 00:29.600
you need to have a really good understanding of where they are.

9
00:30.250 --> 00:33.030
Not just who they are — that’s important, of course —

10
00:33.610 --> 00:37.257
but where they are in terms of their feelings, their understanding,

11
00:37.257 --> 00:39.870
their attitudes, their behaviours to your topic;

12
00:39.870 --> 00:45.150
what pressures might your audience be facing, both professionally and, in many cases, personally?

13
00:45.540 --> 00:48.260
Think about what’s going on in their lives and how that might affect

14
00:48.580 --> 00:50.497
the way they react and respond to you.

15
00:50.497 --> 00:53.220
— And I think important here is making no assumptions.

16
00:53.640 --> 00:57.730
It’s really thinking about what you know about the audience and what they care about.

17
00:58.920 --> 01:02.980
And once we’ve thought about Empathy, we can then think about Purpose, the P of EPIC.

18
01:03.560 --> 01:07.138
And by purpose we mean…

19
01:07.138 --> 01:11.650
the objective of the meeting.

20
01:12.420 --> 01:15.810
Well, for truly impactful communication you need to do more than that.

21
01:16.330 --> 01:19.089
You need to have a clear sense of where you’re going to end up

22
01:19.089 --> 01:22.337
at the end of the meeting, the presentation, whatever the interaction is.

23
01:22.337 --> 01:28.656
And once we’ve thought about Empathy and Purpose, then we think about what we’re going to present at the meeting, or what we’re going to show.

24
01:28.656 --> 01:30.970
And this is the I of EPIC, which stands for Insight.

25
01:31.110 --> 01:37.090
Insight is really all the knowledge and analysis and benchmarking — whatever information you have — that has

26
01:37.480 --> 01:41.063
prompted you to have this meeting, or be called to this meeting, in the first place.

27
01:41.063 --> 01:42.470
The question to ask yourself here

28
01:42.820 --> 01:45.686
is: what insights will serve your purpose?

29
01:45.686 --> 01:49.440
What insights will achieve your purpose in the meeting?

30
01:49.890 --> 01:54.323
Now, you’ve been asked to this meeting because you know a lot about the topic.

31
01:54.323 --> 01:56.710
If communication is more art than science,

32
01:57.320 --> 01:59.430
then it’s more sculpture than painting.

33
02:00.040 --> 02:03.170
In painting, you’re adding to build your picture;

34
02:03.560 --> 02:05.540
in sculpting, you’re chipping away.

35
02:06.020 --> 02:11.483
And when you’re deciding on the insights to use, you’re chipping away at everything you have

36
02:11.483 --> 02:15.520
to reveal the core key insights that will best achieve your purpose.

37
02:15.910 --> 02:19.140
And then finally, the C of EPIC is Conversation.

38
02:19.720 --> 02:23.067
And this is really thinking about how you are going to run the discussion.

39
02:23.067 --> 02:25.600
How will you open it? How will you close the discussion?

40
02:25.990 --> 02:30.240
What roles will people play in the meeting? How might you answer questions people have?

41
02:30.240 --> 02:33.024
And what kind of tone do you want to start and finish on?

42
02:33.024 --> 02:37.127
Whether you’re giving a presentation to a couple of hundred people in an auditorium,

43
02:37.127 --> 02:41.670
whether you’re having a one-on-one, or whether you’re presenting to your executive committee,

44
02:42.310 --> 02:43.691
it’s about conversation.

45
02:43.691 --> 02:47.430
It’s also thinking about how you will interact with the audience.

46
02:48.230 --> 02:51.460
It’s called conversation, not monologue, for a reason.

47
02:52.550 --> 02:54.790
How do you prompt questions?

48
02:55.400 --> 02:58.590
How do you get the audience involved in your content?

49
02:59.140 --> 03:03.820
The EPIC worksheet lays out the questions to ask yourself as you’re preparing.

50
03:03.820 --> 03:07.300
If you use EPIC to plan the last few feet of your journey,

51
03:07.850 --> 03:11.360
then your communication can ensure that what you’ve been working on

52
03:11.780 --> 03:13.630
can have the impact that it needs to have.

